   |  P·R·O·G·R·A·M   D·E·S·C·R·I·P·T·I·O·N
_ _¦__________________________________________________ __ _
   :
 noC-WHO is the one of most full-featured and flexible WHO doors available,
 from  both  the standpoint of the SYSOP and the USER.  Following is a list
 of the available features of this release:
 
   · SIMPLE TO INSTALL: Just drag the drawer into "DOORS:".
   · Compatible with Ami-Express V3.3X+ (32 node version),
     3.2X- (10 node version), and Sigma Express.
   · 9 DESIGN STYLES are included for the user to choose from.
   · All styles are fully EDITABLE/CREATABLE.
   · Sysops may appoint PRIVILEGED users who can:
        · Set their action string.
        · See ALL nodes/fields.
        · Constantly monitor nodes using the update option.
   · noC-WHO may serve MULTIPLE purposes, (frontdoor, etc...)
   · No more 'IN A DOOR' texts.  All doors may be recognized/named.
   · A miscellaneous field may be configured by the sysop.
   · All action texts are configurable.
   · noC-WHO knows when OTHERS are using it.
   · Calls per day are known WITHOUT any additional doors, and are
     reset at midnight automatically.
   · Given the Blitter 'Lick of Approval'
                                                                           :
_ _:_______________________________________________________________________|__
   ¦                                                                       ¦
   |  I·N·S·T·A·L·L·A·T·I·O·N                                              :
_ _¦________________________________________________ __ _
   :

 noC-WHO  is  compatible with Ami-Express versions 2.xx thru 3.xx and Sigma
 Express.    Follow  the  conventional  door  installation methods for your
 version.    Then, simply  copy the "noC-WHO" directory, (found in the path
 "noC-WHO1.5/doors/" when  you  dearchived this package), into "DOORS:" and
 the door is ready to be used.

 To  define  the  available  settings  for  noC-WHO,  edit  the   text file
 "DOORS:noC-WHO/who.config".    All  of  the  OPTIONS and DESIGN-STYLES are
 located  in  this  file.    The following parameters may be set within the
 configuration file:

_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: FILENAMES_DISABLED=[YES/NO]
  Example: FILENAMES_DISABLED=YES 

 If  you  want  that  filenames  NOT be listed with all upload and download
 operations, set this to YES.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: DIVIDERS_DISABLED=[YES/NO]
  Example: DIVIDERS_DISABLED=YES

 If  you have more than 6 nodes or so, you may want to DISABLE the DIVIDING
 LINES between node texts to make the display more COMPACT.  If this is the
 case, set this parameter to YES.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: PUBLIC_UPDATE=[YES/NO]
  Example: PUBLIC_UPDATE=NO

 Some  sysops may not want the general public to be able to use the 'WHO U'
 option  to  CONSTANTLY  MONITOR  other  nodes,  and can use this option to
 DISABLE it.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: DOOR_NAMES=[YES/NO]
  Example: DOOR_NAMES=YES

 If  this option is set to YES, noC-WHO will replace the "IN A DOOR" action
 text  with  the  name of the door a user is in.  In order for this to work
 you must have the door-log turned on for each node.

 If you want that full names be used, (ie "NewFiles V1.5" rather than "N"),
 use the "[DOOR].NAME" option.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: CLEAR_SCREEN=[YES/NO]
  Example: CLEAR_SCREEN=YES

 If  this  is  set  to  YES,  noC-WHO  will  simply clear the screen before
 displaying the node information.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: COOLUSER.[NUM]=[USERNAME]
  Example: COOLUSER.1=nocTurne
           COOLUSER.2=/\donis

 Trusted  users should be listed with this parameter.  They will be able to
 see  EVERYTHING  and  have privileged access to the door.  As each user is
 added you must increment [NUM].  Up to 20 users may be listed.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: MISC.TITLE=[TEXT]
  Example: MISC.TITLE=Phone #

 noC-WHO  features  a  configurable column which you may use as you please.
 (Ideas: Phone numbers, modem types, node info, etc..) To define this field
 for your OWN purposes you must first create a title. 
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: MISC.[NUM]=[TEXT]
  Example: MISC.0=LOCAL
           MISC.1=555-1212
           MISC.2=555-1213

 You may define what is printed in the configurable field of EACH NODE.  If
 you want any of them to display the BAUD, set those to '@'.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: SYSOP_ACTION=[TEXT]
  Example: SYSOP_ACTION=Hanging in the John. 

 Some  sysops  like  to  have  themselves  listed  on  a  local node.  This
 parameter  allows them to customize exactly what their ACTION field says. 
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: YOURNODE.TEXT=[TEXT]
  Example: YOURNODE.TEXT=>> Your Node <<

 The  text  listed  here will be used in place of the user's name, when HIS
 node is being shown to him.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: SOMEONE.TEXT=[TEXT]
  Example: SOMEONE.TEXT=New Caller

 This  text  will be used as the name of a person just logging in or who is
 registering with the system.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: [DOOR].NAME=[TEXT]
  Example: COUP.NAME=noC-Coupon V1.0
           N.NAME=NewFiles V2.0
          
 If the DOOR_NAMES option is on and the door logs are active, you may give
 specific  names  to  each of your doors.  Normally if the user is in, say,
 noC-Coupon,  only the CALLED name will be shown in the action field: COUP.
 If  you want a more  SPECIFIC representation, use the first example shown. 
 Then the action field would read "noC-Coupon V1.0".
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: [CALL].STYLE=[NUM]
  Example: FRONTEND.STYLE=6

 If  you  want  a  specific  style  to be used when the door is called by a
 particular  name,  such  as in the case of a login who display (FRONTEND),
 you may select the style to be used with this option.  (In the example, if
 noC-WHO  is  called  by  the name FRONTEND, it will use the 6th design, [A
 compact node display]).  If you want that the user NOT have the ability to
 select  a  design,  you  can  SET  the style by using this command.  (eg. 
 WHO.STYLE=1)
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 If  you  want  to  customize  the  action  texts  you  can  edit the texts
 proceeding the "@BEGIN_ACTIONS" string in the included config file.  These
 are  the  default  texts  for  every action, (Awaiting call, Beginning UL,
 etc...).    When  you  EDIT  these  texts,  be sure to keep them around 18
 characters  long  and  KEEP  THEM  IN THE ORDER THEY CAME.  Any additional
 carriage  returns or alterations to the arrangement of the file may result
 in incorrect action displays.  "@END_ACTIONS" terminates the action list.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: TITLE.[NUM]=[TEXT]
  Example: TITLE.1=Simple Design

 Use  this  string  to designate a NAME for a particular who design.  [NUM]
 may be a number from 1 to 9, as you may have up to 9 screen-styles for the
 user  to  select  from.   The title will be shown during the CONFIGURATION
 session  and  should  convey  the  'MOOD'  of its respective design, while
 remaining under 18 characters.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: HEAD.[NUM]=[TEXT]%
  Example: 
-----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------
 HEAD.1=
 =========================================================
 NODE      NAME                      @MISC..........  CONF
 =========================================================
 %
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------

 This  defines  the  HEADER  for  the  design number [NUM].  This text will
 comprise  the  top  part  of  the  who screen and may be used to title the
 fields you  will define in the BODY.[NUM] section,  or it may simply be an
 interesting  looking  design to preceed the node texts.  ANSI codes may be
 used  anywhere.  Look at one of the 9 examples included for more examples.
 Note that the text may have as many carriage returns as you like, but must
 be TERMINATED by a '%' sign.

 See "Header/Tail Commands" for the '@' commands available.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: BODY.[NUM]=[TEXT]%
  Example: 
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------
 BODY.1=
  @#       @NAME.................... @MISC..........   @$
 %
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------

 The  text  written  after  this  argument will compose the BODY of the who
 display  [NUM].   This text will repeatedly be displayed for each node and
 information  will be filled in accordingly.  As with all design texts, the
 body-text  must  be terminated by a '%', and may be composed of any number
 of ANSI codes.

 See "Body Commands" for the '@' commands available.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: DIVIDER.[NUM]=[TEXT]%
  Example: 
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------
 DIVIDER.1=
 (======================================================)
 %
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------

 If  you  have  node  dividers enabled, the text written here will separate
 each  of  the  body(node)  texts.    You  may  use as many rows of text to
 separate the node informations; however, one row is suggested.  Again, the
 text must end with a '%' and may be composed of ANSI sequences.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

 Template: TAIL.[NUM]=[TEXT]%
  Example: 
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------
 TAIL.1=
 (======================================================)
  @VERSIONTEXT - (C)1994 nocTurne deSign - Time: @TIME
 %
 -----------------------8< Cut Here -------------------------------

 This  text  will  be  the last string emitted.  You may use it to show the
 time,  the number of calls for the day, or to give yourself credit for the
 design  you  created.  Any number of lines may be used, ANSI sequences are
 allowed, and the text must be terminated with a '%'.

 See "Header/Tail Commands" for the '@' commands available.
                                                                             ¦
_ _:_________________________________________________________________________|__
   ¦                                                                         ¦
   |  P·R·O·G·R·A·M   L·O·G·I·C                                              :
_ _¦__________________________________________________ __ _
   :

 Information  on  program usage may be obtained with "WHO ?".  To CONFIGURE
 noC-WHO  to  one of the nine styles available, the user may enter "WHO E",
 which  will prompt him to select his preference.  A DATA FILE with all the
 users'  preferences will be kept named "DOORS:noC-WHO/data/who-users.dat".

 If  the UPDATE option has been made available to all users, or the user is
 privileged,  he may use this door to actively monitor the actions of those
 online.    "WHO  U"  will cause noC-WHO to update its display every second
 until  a  key  is pressed.  If the "PUBLIC_UPDATE" config option is set to
 "NO",  only  privileged users will be able to have display updates.  Those
 with  privileged  access  may  also  set their ACTION text.  Setting it to
 NOTHING will cause all actions to be displayed normally.

 Nodes  that   are   QUIETED   are   EXEMPT   from   being   displayed   to
 'underpriviledged'  users.  Nodes that are disabled or unavailable will be
 shown as such.  At midnight, a task spawned by the door upon its first run
 will AUTOMATICALLY RESET the call counter to 0.
                                                                             ¦
_ _:_________________________________________________________________________|__
   ¦                                                                         ¦
   |  C·O·M·M·A·N·D  S·E·T  E·X·P·L·A·N·A·T·I·O·N                            :
_ _¦__________________________________________________ __ _
   :

 noC-WHO  features  an  '@'  command set, (each command begins with the '@'
 character),  which  may  be  used  to  call  up particular strings in your
 header, body, and tail texts.  This is best explained with examples:

 Take  the  '@TIME' command.  When noC-WHO finds this text in the header or
 tail texts, it will replace it with the current system time.

 If your tail text in the config file reads:
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------
 ===============================================================
  @VERSIONTEXT - This WHO-Door Smells - The Time: @TIME
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------

 When displayed it would read:
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------
 ===============================================================
  noC-WHO V1.50 - This WHO-Door Smells - The Time: 01:20
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------

 You  may  use  periods  to extend the length of the text.  For example, to
 display the system time WITH seconds:

 "The Time: @TIME..."-----=would yield=----->"The Time: 01:20:12"

 An example from the body-text command set; if this were your body string:

 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------
  @# | @NAME............... | @LOC................... | @ACTION.......
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------

 The output text (body only, for three nodes) would then be:

 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------
  01 | nocTurne             | ·-÷« MST^CoDeR »÷-·     | NUN'A'YOR'BIZ!
  02 |                      |                         | Awaiting Call 
  03 | Jake Steinfeld       | From body-building heav | Viewing Dirs  
 ----------------------------------------------8< Cut Here -----------

 As  you  can  see from node 3, where there are no periods the text will be
 cut  off.    If  you  only used "@NAME", without any of the periods, names
 would extend no longer than 5 characters.

 This concludes your lesson of the noC-WHO command-set.
                                                                             ¦
_ _:_________________________________________________________________________|__
   ¦                                                                         ¦
   |  H·E·A·D·E·R / T·A·I·L   C·O·M·M·A·N·D·S                                :
_ _¦__________________________________________________ __ _
   :

  Command: @CL
   Result: Number of calls today

 If  you  expect more than a thousand calls in one day (!), you may want to
 extend the field to 4 characters, ("@CL.").
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @MISC
   Result: Text defined in MISC.TITLE config parameter

 This  string  will  be  replaced with the title of the miscellaneous field
 you've  defined.  ie,  whatever has  been defined in the MISC.TITLE config
 argument will be displayed here.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @TIME
   Result: System time

 By  default,  a five character time display will replace the command text,
 ("@TIME"  is  five characters long).  Thus only the hours and seconds will
 be  shown.  Extending the field to eight characters will show the complete
 system time, (ie "@TIME..."--->"01:12:20").
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @VERSIONTEXT
   Result: "noC-WHO V1.5" string

 This  command  text  will  be replaced with the version string of noC-WHO.
 THIS  COMMAND  MUST  BE  SOMEWHERE  IN  YOUR  HEADER/TAIL  TEXT or else an
 annoying  version string will be appended to the who display.  Be warned.   :
                                                                             ¦
_ _:_________________________________________________________________________|__
   ¦                                                                         ¦
   |  B·O·D·Y   C·O·M·M·A·N·D·S                                              :
_ _¦__________________________________________________ __ _
   :

  Command: @#
   Result: Current node number being showed

 This is simply a two character field that will be replaced with the number
 of  the  NODE  that  is currently being displayed.  As each node is shown,
 this number will be incremented respectively.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @$
   Result: User's current conference

 This command will show the current node's user CONFERENCE NUMBER.   Please
 note,  in  order  for this to function correctly you MUST HAVE THE CALLERS
 LOG for each node TURNED ON.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @ACTION
   Result: What the user is doing

 This  string  will reflect what ACTION the user of the node being shown is
 taking.    It  is  suggested  you extend the field to around 18 characters
 length, (ie "@ACTION...........").
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @BAUD
   Result: Users connect rate

 This  string  will  be  replaced with the BAUD of the user currently being
 shown.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @LOC
   Result: User's Location/Group

 This  field  should be extended to around 16 characters to fit the average
 user's location line, (ie, "@LOC............")
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @MISC
   Result: Misc field definition for current node

 This command will show what is defined in the MISC field for each node.
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @NAME
   Result: The user's name/handle

 To  show  most user's full names/handles, this field should be extended to
 around 14 characters, (ie, "@NAME.........").
_ __ __________________________________________________________________ __ _

  Command: @WHEN
   Result: User's time of login

 This  will  show  the  time,  (hours  and minutes), of the currently shown
 user's  login.  If you wish seconds to be shown as well, this field should
 be  extended to 8 characters, (ie, "@WHEN...").  YOU MUST HAVE THE CALLERS
 LOG ON FOR THIS COMMAND TO FUNCTION.                                        :
